--TEST--
A `mixed` or `?T` parameter of an anonymous function admits every argument, so a Core\Arr callback declaring one is never refused while its non-nullable twin always is — `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:types/arrays`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The depth claim is *a bound asserted on both sides*. `nvs_runtime` checks a
// callable's arguments against the tags the callable declared, and the tag it
// compares is the parameter's **representation**, not its declared type: a
// `?T` and a `mixed` both erase to one tagged slot, which admits every tag by
// construction (`nvs_ir::lower::param_tag_nibble`'s own doc comment owns why).
// So "unchecked" is the answer here rather than a hole, and the way to pin an
// answer that consists of nothing happening is to name the refusal it sits
// next to: each declaration below is written twice, bare and nullable, over
// the same member and the same argument. A check that read the declared type
// would refuse both halves of a pair; a check that had been dropped would
// accept both. Only "the representation is the check" prints this line.
//
// The keys are `rule:types/arrays`'s `string`, over the packed list the neighbouring
// `arr-a-callback-key-is-a-string-on-a-list-too` pins, so every refusal below
// is a `string` argument meeting a parameter of some other representation.
//
// Every question is asked through an option bag's `{by:}`, which is a bare
// `callable`. A member whose callback is a whole parameter writes its
// signature (`rule:types/callable-signature`) and answers most of these before
// the run, so it cannot show what the representation check does.

array<int> $list = [10, 20, 30, 40];
array<int> $map = ["a" => 1, "b" => 2];

// One character per declaration, in pairs: the bare form then its nullable
// twin. `y` is "the member ran", `n` is "the call threw `LogicError`".
var $marks = "";
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, int $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, ?int $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, bool $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, ?bool $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, array<string> $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, ?array<string> $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
// The pair the key's own representation is on: both halves accept, one
// because the tag matches and one because nothing looked.
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, string $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, ?string $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
// `mixed` is `rule:types/conversion`'s one unchecked position and has no bare twin.
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(int $v, mixed $k) => true}); $marks = $marks . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $marks = $marks . "n"; }
echo $marks, "\n";

// The same pair in the *value* position, so the rule is about an argument
// rather than about the key: an `int` element meeting a `string` parameter.
var $value = "";
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(string $v, string $k) => true}); $value = $value . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $value = $value . "n"; }
try { Core\Arr::unique($list, {by: fn(?string $v, string $k) => true}); $value = $value . "y"; }
catch (LogicError $e) { $value = $value . "n"; }
echo $value, "\n";

// And the argument arrives intact under a declaration that disagrees with it:
// `$k as string` inside a `?int $k` reads the tag the value actually carries,
// so the keys come back whole rather than converted, defaulted or nulled.
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::countBy($list, {by: fn(int $v, ?int $k) => $k as string})), ","), "\n";
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::countBy($map, {by: fn(int $v, ?int $k) => $k as string})), ","), "\n";
// Both parameters wrong at once: the `?bool $v` holds the `int` element, and
// renders as one. Two arguments rather than three, because the three-argument
// callback — `reduce`'s — writes its signature now, and every position that
// still reaches the representation check is offered `($value, $key)`.
echo Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::keys(Core\Arr::countBy($map,
    {by: fn(?bool $v, mixed $k) => ($k as string) . ($v as string)})), ","), "\n";
--EXPECT--
nynynyyyy
ny
0,1,2,3
a,b
a1,b2
